# Repository Governance

**{{ org_name }}** | EIN: {{ ein }} | {{ state }} 501(c)(3){% if charter_no %} | Charter No. {{ charter_no }}{% endif %}

This document defines how this repository is governed. It is the first document any board member, auditor, grant officer, or partner should read to understand how institutional decisions are proposed, debated, adopted, amended, and preserved. It operationalizes the bylaws for a git-native governance workflow.

---

## 1. Purpose

This repository is the **official record of all adopted governance documents** for {{ org_name }}, a{{ ' federal-grant-focused' if federal_grants_focus else '' }} 501(c)(3) public charity incorporated in {{ state }} under {{ incorp_statute }}.

The `main` branch of this repository constitutes the **current official state of the institution**. Any document on `main` is, by definition, in force. Any document not on `main` is not in force. There is no other canonical source.

This repository holds:

- The bylaws{% if not include_bylaws_example %} (this configuration does not include the commons' worked bylaws example — add your organization's adopted bylaws here directly, see `docs/NOT-LEGAL-ADVICE.md` in the commons for why this template does not attempt to write them for you by default){% endif %}
- Board-adopted policies and standing rules
- Board resolutions and voting records
- Approved meeting minutes
- Executed agreements, MOUs, and subawards
- Tax filings, state filings, and compliance artifacts
- Board-approved templates and forms

Working drafts, agendas, meeting preparation, AI agent scratch space, and deliberative material **do not belong here**. If your organization maintains a separate working/drafting repository, keep that material there — see `CLAUDE.md`, Section "Relationship to a Working/Drafting Repository."

---

## 2. Principles

The governance model rests on six invariants. They are non-negotiable.

1. **Each policy is a file. Each amendment is a commit. Each adoption is a merge.** The unit of governance is the file; the unit of change is the commit; the unit of authority is the merge to `main`.
2. **Git history is the immutable audit trail.** Every change to every document is cryptographically linked and timestamped. `git log` is the minute book. `git blame` is the chain of custody.
3. **{{ mr_term | capitalize }} approval by board members constitutes valid written consent** under the organization's bylaws (see your bylaws' electronic-voting or written-consent provisions). The {{ mr_abbr }} is the motion. Approvals are the votes. The merge is the adoption.
4. **The merge commit timestamp is the official adoption date.** Not the date written in the document. Not the date of the meeting. The git timestamp on `main` is authoritative for legal, tax, and regulatory purposes.
5. **No direct pushes to `main`. Ever.** Branch protection is enforced at the {{ platform_name }} project level. Any bypass of this rule — whether by force-push, history rewrite, or administrative override — is a governance violation and must be reported to the full Board and recorded in the next minutes.
6. **Placeholder or synthetic material is prohibited in operative records.** Fillable blanks are allowed only in `templates/`. Incomplete or synthetic examples must not be presented as adopted records.

---

## 3. Document Lifecycle

Every governance document moves through a defined sequence of states. The state is recorded in the document's YAML frontmatter under `status`.

```
draft → proposed → review → adopted → amended → superseded → archived
```

| State | Meaning | Location |
|-------|---------|----------|
| `draft` | Working document being prepared. Not yet submitted for consideration. | Your working/drafting space, if any |
| `proposed` | Submitted to this repo via an open {{ mr_abbr }} on a `board/` or `admin/` branch. The text should already be in final in-force wording. | This repo, feature branch |
| `review` | {{ mr_abbr }} is open and in the mandatory review period. Merge activates the exact reviewed text. | This repo, feature branch |
| `adopted` | Merged to `main` with the required approvals. In force. | This repo, `main` |
| `amended` | A newer version has been adopted on `main`. This version's frontmatter has `superseded_by` pointing to the new `id`. Still present in git history. | This repo, `main` and history |
| `superseded` | Fully replaced by a successor document. May be moved into an archive subdirectory or retained in place with updated frontmatter. | This repo, `main` or archive |
| `archived` | No longer in effect. Retained solely for historical and audit reference. | This repo, `main` under archive subdirectory |

A document never moves backward through the lifecycle. A superseded policy is not re-adopted; a new version is proposed and passes through the full cycle on its own merits.

---

## 4. Who Can Propose Changes

The right to propose is deliberately broad. The right to adopt is deliberately narrow.

- **Any director or officer** may propose a change by creating a branch in this repo and opening {{ mr_article }} {{ mr_term }} here.
- **Committee chairs** may propose changes within the scope of their committee's charter.
{%- if ai_agents_enabled %}
- **AI agents** authorized by the Board Chair may prepare {{ mr_term }}s, draft frontmatter, assemble supporting materials, and carry out mechanical edits. However, **a human director or officer must be the {{ mr_abbr }} author of record, or must explicitly endorse the {{ mr_abbr }} in writing in its description.** AI agents do not hold votes and cannot author governance actions unattended — see `CLAUDE.md` for the full hard rule.
{%- endif %}
- **External parties** (grant officers, auditors, partners, counsel) do not have write access and cannot propose changes directly. Their input is routed through a director or officer who opens the {{ mr_abbr }} on their behalf.

---

## 5. Branch Naming

Branch prefixes are load-bearing. They determine the approval tier required for merge. **This scheme is fixed** — it is an interoperability convention shared across every organization using this commons, and is not a Copier variable.

| Prefix | Approval Required | Scope |
|--------|-------------------|-------|
| `board/` | **{{ board_quorum_prose | capitalize }}**{% if board_quorum_count %} — with a {{ board_size }}-seat board, that is currently **{{ board_quorum_count }}** director(s){% endif %} | Bylaws, policies, resolutions, elections, officer appointments, board roster changes, amendments to this `GOVERNANCE.md` |
| `admin/` | **1 officer** (Chair, Executive Director, Secretary, or {{ treasurer_title }} as appropriate) | Minutes, financial reports, tax filings, state filings, routine correspondence, template typo fixes |

### Format

Branch names must follow one of:

- `board/YYYY-QN-<topic>` for quarterly meeting omnibus {{ mr_abbr }}s — e.g. `board/2026-q3-meeting`
- `board/YYYY-MM-<topic>` for single-subject board actions — e.g. `board/2026-08-travel-policy-amendment`
- `admin/YYYY-QN-<topic>` or `admin/YYYY-MM-<topic>` — e.g. `admin/2026-q3-minutes`

### Branch Hygiene

- Branches are **deleted after merge**. The git history preserves the work; the branch reference is no longer needed.
- Branches must not be reused across unrelated changes. One branch, one coherent proposal.
- Long-lived feature branches are prohibited for governance work. If a proposal stalls, close the {{ mr_abbr }} and archive the branch.

---

## 6. {{ mr_term | capitalize }} Process

The {{ mr_term }} is the motion. It is prepared, discussed, voted on, and either adopted or rejected — all within {{ platform_name }}, all preserved in the permanent record.

### Required {{ mr_abbr }} Contents

Every {{ mr_abbr }} against this repository must include:

1. **The motion or resolution text** — the formal language being put to the board, in the {{ mr_abbr }} description.
2. **Affected documents** — an explicit list of every file added, modified, or removed.
3. **Legal basis** — the bylaws section, prior resolution, statute, or regulation that authorizes this action.
4. **Effective date** — when the change takes effect if adopted (may be the merge date itself, or a future date specified in the document frontmatter).
5. **Review period classification** — whether this is a standard, emergency, or administrative action, per your bylaws.

The {{ mr_abbr }} template at `{{ review_template_path }}` enforces these fields. Use it.

### Review Period

- **Policy changes** (`board/` branches): the review period set by your bylaws (commonly 7 days), waivable only by unanimous written consent of all directors then serving for documented emergencies.
- **Bylaw amendments**: the review period and vote threshold set by your bylaws (a supermajority is common practice for bylaw amendments even where ordinary board actions use a lower threshold — see `bylaws.md` if this configuration includes the commons' worked example, or your organization's own adopted bylaws).
- **Administrative {{ mr_abbr }}s** (`admin/`): no minimum period; may be merged as soon as the required officer approves.

### Discussion Record

- **{{ mr_abbr }} comments serve as the board discussion record.** They are preserved indefinitely and are part of the corporate books.
- **All open threads must be resolved before merge.** An unresolved thread is an unresolved objection.
- **Directors and officers must use their real identities** — no anonymous or pseudonymous comments on governance {{ mr_abbr }}s. {{ platform_name }} accounts must be tied to director identity verified at the time of seating.

### Merge Rules

- **No squash merges.** Full commit history is preserved. Each author's contribution remains attributable. This is a bright-line rule — {{ 'squash is disabled at the project level for this repository' if vcs_platform == 'gitlab' else 'the "Squash and merge" and "Rebase and merge" options are disabled for this repository, leaving only "Create a merge commit"' }}.
- **No force pushes to any branch after {{ mr_article }} {{ mr_abbr }} is opened.** Rewriting proposed history during review destroys the audit trail of the deliberation itself.
- **Fast-forward or merge commit only.** The merge commit is the adoption record and must be preserved.
- **The merge commit message must reference the {{ mr_abbr }} number** and cite the motion being adopted.

---

## 7. Voting

Voting happens in {{ platform_name }}. There is no separate ballot unless your organization has separately adopted one (see "Integrity Rules" below).

| Action in {{ platform_name }} | Equivalent in Robert's Rules |
|------------------|------------------------------|
| Approve {{ mr_abbr }} | Aye |
| Request Changes | Motion to debate; must specify concerns in {{ mr_article }} {{ mr_abbr }} comment |
| Leave a comment without approving | Discussion; not yet a vote |
| Silence through the full review period | Abstain |
| Close the {{ mr_abbr }} without merging | Motion withdrawn or defeated |

### Thresholds

- **`board/` branches**: approval by {{ board_quorum_prose }} for ordinary board actions.{% if board_quorum_count %} With the current {{ board_size }}-seat board, that means {{ board_quorum_count }} director(s), unless the bylaws require a higher threshold for a specific action (e.g. bylaw amendments).{% endif %}
- **`admin/` branches**: 1 officer approval, provided the officer holds authority over the subject matter under the Delegation of Authority Policy.
- **Bylaw amendments**: the supermajority threshold set by your bylaws.

### Integrity Rules

- **Director-authored {{ mr_abbr }}s are allowed.** A director-author's approval counts as that director's consent. Service, bot, or admin accounts may open or maintain {{ mr_article }} {{ mr_abbr }}, but they do not count toward director quorum and must not be the only named proposer of a board action.
- **Approvals are reset on substantive push.** If the {{ mr_abbr }} author pushes material changes after an approval, that approval is invalidated and must be renewed. This is enforced by {{ platform_name }}'s "remove approvals on new commits" (or equivalent) setting.
- **If your organization uses a separate electronic-voting platform** in parallel with {{ platform_name }} approvals for certain actions (e.g. formal elections), the {{ mr_abbr }} description must reference that platform's record ID, and both records must agree. [Specify in your bylaws which record controls in case of conflict.]

