## 9. YAML Frontmatter Standard

Every document in this repository must begin with a YAML frontmatter block. The block is machine-readable and is the source of truth for document metadata.

### Required Fields

```yaml
---
id: POL-001                        # Sequential identifier within the document type
type: policy                       # policy | bylaws | resolution | template | governance | agreement | compliance
organization: {{ org_name }}
title: "Document Title"
version: "1.0"                     # Semver; increment for substantive changes
status: adopted                    # draft | proposed | review | adopted | amended | superseded | archived
effective_date: on-merge           # Use on-merge unless the document has a delayed future effective date
adopted_meeting: <to be set on adoption>  # Identifier linking to the resolutions/ file or meeting record
legal_basis: "Citation"            # Bylaws section, statute, regulation, or prior resolution
supersedes: null                   # ID of a prior document this replaces, or null
superseded_by: null                # ID of a successor document, or null
review_cycle: annual               # annual | biennial | as-needed
next_review: <to be set on adoption>      # Next scheduled review
---
```

### Identifier Conventions

- `GOV-NNN` — governance documents (this file, charter amendments)
- `BYL-NNN` — bylaws articles (when versioned separately)
- `POL-NNN` — policies
- `RES-NNN` — resolutions
- `TMPL-NNN` — templates
- `MIN-YYYY-QN` — minutes
- `VOT-YYYY-NNN` — voting records

Identifiers are assigned sequentially within their type and are never reused, even if a document is archived.

### Frontmatter as Contract

When `status: adopted` appears on a `board/` branch, the file is staged in its final in-force wording. It becomes operative when that exact file is merged to `main`. Merging a file with `status: draft` to `main` is a governance violation and must be corrected by a follow-up {{ mr_abbr }} within a short, board-defined window.

